# Template for .env.1password — 1Password secret references consumed by `op run`.
#
# The op:// references below are NOT secret values, but they do contain
# environment-specific metadata (your vault / item names or ids), so the real
# file is git-ignored and only this sample is committed.
#
# Setup (once per machine):
#   1. Copy this file:
#        bash:       cp QuantConnect.BitbankBrokerage/env.1password.sample QuantConnect.BitbankBrokerage/.env.1password
#        PowerShell: Copy-Item QuantConnect.BitbankBrokerage/env.1password.sample QuantConnect.BitbankBrokerage/.env.1password
#   2. Find your item (see SETUP.md §2 for creating it):
#        op vault list
#        op item list --vault <vault>
#   3. Check its field ids (labels differ by item category / locale):
#        op item get <item-name-or-id> --format json
#      The "API Credential" category uses field ids: username / credential.
#   4. Edit the two references below. Format: op://<vault>/<item name or id>/<field id>
#      Using the item ID (not the title) keeps the reference stable across renames.
#
# Verify without exposing values:
#   op run --env-file=QuantConnect.BitbankBrokerage/.env.1password -- powershell -NoProfile -Command 'Write-Output ($env:BITBANK_API_KEY).Length'
#
# Usage (from the repo root):
#   op run --env-file=QuantConnect.BitbankBrokerage/.env.1password -- <command>
BITBANK_API_KEY="op://<vault>/<item-id>/username"
BITBANK_API_SECRET="op://<vault>/<item-id>/credential"
